ords ''Twin-kle twin-kle lit-tle star'' have the rhythm ta ta ta ta ta ta taa (one syllable per beat, with the last one held for two beats). A song with ''ti-ti ta'' would have two short sounds on one beat and then one long one. The beat never changes, but the rhythm can.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSay the words ''pizza and pasta'' while tapping a steady beat. Which words fall on one beat each, and which words share a beat?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Piz-za and pas-ta: piz-za (ti-ti) share one beat, and (ta) takes one beat, then pas-ta (ti-ti).'' So the rhythm is ti-ti ta ti-ti, over a steady beat. The beat stays even while the words make the rhythm.","explain":"The beat is the steady pulse in music, like a heartbeat or a clock ticking. It stays the same while the melody changes. The rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over the beat. A quarter note, said ''ta'', lasts one beat, a pair of eighth notes, said ''ti-ti'', share one beat and a quarter rest, a silent beat, is shown with a small symbol. You can tap the beat and clap the rhythm of a song at the same time to hear the difference.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that the beat and the rhythm are the same thing. The beat is the steady pulse that does not change, and the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over it. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Beat and rhythm.","caption":"Beat stays, rhythm changes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">steady tap tap tap tap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">ta ta ti-ti ta</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: in ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words ''Twin-kle twi Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: in ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words ''Twin-kle twi Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a steady beat with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: in ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words ''Twin-kle twin-kle lit-tle star'' have the rhythm ta ta ta ta ta ta taa (one syllable per beat, with the last one held for two beats). A song with ''ti-ti ta'' would have two short sounds on one beat and then one long one. The beat never changes, but the rhythm can. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Ta and ti-ti notes.","caption":"Ta is one, ti-ti is two.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">ta</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">ti-ti</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">two sounds in one beat</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a steady beat with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that the beat and the rhythm are the same thing. The beat is the steady pulse that does not change, and the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over it. Here is a more helpful example: Example: in ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words ''Twin-kle twin-kle lit-tle star'' have the rhythm ta ta ta ta ta ta taa (one syllable per beat, with the last one held for two beats). A song with ''ti-ti ta'' would have two short sounds on one beat and then one long one. The beat never changes, but the rhythm can.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Piz-za and pas-ta: piz-za (ti-ti) share one beat, and (ta) takes one beat, then pas-ta (ti-ti).'' So the rhythm is ti-ti ta ti-ti, over a steady beat. The beat stays even while the words make the rhythm.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that the beat and the rhythm are the same thing. The beat is the steady pulse that does not change, and the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over it. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a steady beat with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe beat is the steady pulse in music, like a heartbeat or a clock ticking. It stays the same while the melody changes. The rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over the beat. A quarter note, said ''ta'', lasts one beat, a pair of eighth notes, said ''ti-ti'', share one beat and a quarter rest, a silent beat, is shown with a small symbol. You can tap the beat and clap the rhythm of a song at the same time to hear the difference. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 1: Steady beat and rhythm","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Beat, rhythm and pitch","week":6,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: in ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words ''Twin-kle twin-kle lit-tle star'' have the rhythm ta ta ta ta ta ta taa (one syllable per beat, with the last one held for two beats). A song with ''ti-ti ta'' would have two short sounds on one beat and then one long one. The beat never changes, but the rhythm can.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Beat and rhythm.","caption":"Beat stays, rhythm changes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Beat</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">steady tap tap tap tap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rhythm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">ta ta ti-ti ta</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Ta and ti-ti notes.","caption":"Ta is one, ti-ti is two.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">ta</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one beat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">ti-ti</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">two sounds in one beat</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w06-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
